1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,000
Parity.

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:04,027
Meat price divided by feed price.

3
00:00:04,027 --> 00:00:07,037
How many kilos of feed does one kilo of beef buy?

4
00:00:07,037 --> 00:00:10,310
That single ratio is the fifty-five-year code behind meat prices.

5
00:00:11,010 --> 00:00:13,001
Parity is the whole story here:

6
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:15,120
meat price divided by feed price.

7
00:00:15,120 --> 00:00:18,138
America measures slaughter cattle against corn,

8
00:00:18,138 --> 00:00:21,156
Europe young bull carcasses against feed grain,

9
00:00:21,156 --> 00:00:24,046
Türkiye its meat index against compound feed.

10
00:00:24,046 --> 00:00:25,446
The rulers differ —

11
00:00:25,446 --> 00:00:28,400
so what you see here is not price, but change.

12
00:00:29,170 --> 00:00:31,351
Our oldest data comes from America,

13
00:00:31,351 --> 00:00:32,784
reaching back to 1971 —

14
00:00:32,784 --> 00:00:35,028
so that is where the telling begins.

15
00:00:35,028 --> 00:00:38,268
In seventy-two the Soviets, hiding a failed harvest,

16
00:00:38,268 --> 00:00:41,945
quietly bought nearly a quarter of the American wheat crop,

17
00:00:41,945 --> 00:00:43,440
then the oil crisis hit.

18
00:00:43,440 --> 00:00:46,244
Corn tripled, and the parity hit rock bottom:

19
00:00:46,244 --> 00:00:49,360
a kilo of beef barely covered half a kilo of feed.

20
00:00:50,310 --> 00:00:52,661
Every drought pulls the same chain.

21
00:00:52,661 --> 00:00:56,154
In 2012 the Great Plains burned, feed got expensive,

22
00:00:56,154 --> 00:00:58,169
ranchers slaughtered the herd.

23
00:00:58,169 --> 00:01:00,520
Three years later beef was scarce —

24
00:01:00,520 --> 00:01:03,140
and the parity soared to its 2014 peak.

25
00:01:04,090 --> 00:01:06,731
We stacked four separate crises:

26
00:01:06,731 --> 00:01:08,876
1974, 1996, 2011 and 2021.

27
00:01:08,876 --> 00:01:12,508
Four different decades, one identical curve.

28
00:01:12,508 --> 00:01:15,561
Economists call it the cobweb cycle —

29
00:01:15,561 --> 00:01:20,100
because a slaughtered cow takes three years to replace.

30
00:01:21,050 --> 00:01:24,386
Türkiye joins the screen in 2010, Europe in 2015.

31
00:01:24,386 --> 00:01:25,786
This is not a race;

32
00:01:25,786 --> 00:01:29,463
each country is measured against its own 2016 average.

33
00:01:29,463 --> 00:01:33,549
And today America runs eighty-four percent above that line —

34
00:01:33,549 --> 00:01:36,136
the sweetest territory in its history.

35
00:01:36,980 --> 00:01:38,739
In 2021, for the first time,

36
00:01:38,739 --> 00:01:41,126
the crisis erupted everywhere at once:

37
00:01:41,126 --> 00:01:43,450
pandemic, drought and war stacked up,

38
00:01:43,450 --> 00:01:45,963
and feed prices flew on every continent.

39
00:01:45,963 --> 00:01:47,973
The shock began in the Atlantic:

40
00:01:47,973 --> 00:01:49,544
America hit bottom first;

41
00:01:49,544 --> 00:01:53,061
six months later a currency shock hardened it in Ankara,

42
00:01:53,061 --> 00:01:55,700
and it reached Brussels a year after that.

43
00:01:55,700 --> 00:01:59,720
Feed built on imported inputs prices a currency shock instantly.

44
00:02:00,670 --> 00:02:02,407
So what does the feeder earn?

45
00:02:02,407 --> 00:02:04,205
On a hundred units of revenue,

46
00:02:04,205 --> 00:02:06,182
the calf and overheads are fixed;

47
00:02:06,182 --> 00:02:07,800
feed moves with the parity.

48
00:02:07,800 --> 00:02:10,016
In trough years the table bleeds red:

49
00:02:10,016 --> 00:02:11,694
minus fifty-five in America.

50
00:02:11,694 --> 00:02:14,390
Today, all three continents are in the green.

51
00:02:15,340 --> 00:02:17,456
Who survives the same wave, and how?

52
00:02:17,456 --> 00:02:20,631
The American locks the margin with futures in Chicago.

53
00:02:20,631 --> 00:02:22,747
The European's shield is the budget:

54
00:02:22,747 --> 00:02:24,981
direct payments are a fifth of income.

55
00:02:24,981 --> 00:02:27,391
The Turkish feeder has no futures market,

56
00:02:27,391 --> 00:02:30,330
no margin insurance — and credit at fifty percent.

57
00:02:31,280 --> 00:02:33,159
Fifty-five years, one sentence:

58
00:02:33,159 --> 00:02:35,644
beef is not a choice, it is a cost story.

59
00:02:35,644 --> 00:02:38,674
You cannot shrink the wave — but you can price it.

60
00:02:38,674 --> 00:02:41,220
The full data lives at namikakman dot dev.
